  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067637/assets/251e49513353092b4f9a16f54bb14fbaa1afe1a14e5fecf5b3ce54905fed474d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?",
  "worked_solution": "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=y\\,i+j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=x\\,i,\\,K(x,y)=-i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=y\\,i.$",
